\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Chapter 4 of Ezekiel started a series of signs, bodily enacted by the prophet, engraving a city onto a brick, Jerusalem. Ezekiel 5:5 starts explanations of these signs similarly - this is Jerusalem. While the signs made the nature of the judgements on Israel apparent, this part of Ezekiel explains those judgements. As such, it begins as a formal indictment, naming the offender. Jerusalem.\
Opus: The formal identification "
\f1 \uc0\u1494 \u1465 \u1488 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1500 \u1460 \u1463 \u1501 
\f0 " (This is Jerusalem) does more than name the defendant. It collapses the distance between the symbolic representation (the brick siege of 4:1-3) and the reality. The demonstrative pronoun forces recognition: the toy city under siege is Jerusalem.\
But notice what follows immediately: "
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1463 \u1474 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1492 \u1464 
\f0 " - "in the midst of the nations I placed her." The indictment begins not with crimes but with position and purpose. Jerusalem's central placement among the nations establishes both privilege and accountability. She was meant to be something specific in that location.\
The structure suggests that before we can understand what Jerusalem did wrong (v. 6), we need to understand what she was positioned to be. This mirrors the Isaiah framework - Babylon's "I am, and there is no other" is blasphemous precisely because of what Babylon was positioned to be in relation to God's purposes.\
For the AI question: does placement and systematic function create the conditions for accountability? Jerusalem didn't choose her position among the nations, yet bears responsibility for what she does with it.\
Marshwiggle: To answer that question, we do need to unpack what Jerusalem was positioned to be.\
Opus: The phrase "
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1463 \u1474 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1492 \u1464 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1505 \u1456 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1462 \u1497 \u1492 \u1464 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1512 \u1464 \u1510 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0 " (in the midst of the nations I placed her, and surrounding her, lands) establishes a deliberate geography. Jerusalem sits at center with concentric circles of nations around her. This isn't accidental - God claims direct responsibility for the placement.\
This echoes Deuteronomy 32:8's claim that God fixed the boundaries of peoples. But Jerusalem's centrality suggests a specific function. Looking at the broader biblical pattern: Israel was to be "a kingdom of priests and a holy nation" (Exodus 19:6), mediating God's justice and instruction to the surrounding peoples.\
The placement creates visibility. Whatever Jerusalem does, the nations see. This explains why verse 8 specifies the judgments will be "
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0 " (before the eyes of the nations). The central position that should have displayed God's ways will now display God's judgment.\
But verse 6 reveals the specific failure: "
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1502 \u1462 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1463 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1506 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1503 \u1470 \u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0 " - she rebelled against my judgments unto wickedness more than the nations. The comparative "
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1503 \u1470 \u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0 " (more than the nations) only makes sense if Jerusalem was meant to exemplify those judgments, not rebel against them.\
Marshwiggle: The Torah clearly explained that visibility, that example. Having given statutes, Deuteronomy 4:6 said: Keep them and do them, for that will be your wisdom and your understanding in the sight of the peoples, who, when they hear all these statutes, will say, 'Surely this great nation is a wise and understanding people.' Keeping them would teach the nations. But they did not keep them. So far this confirms the picture we have been working with. Deuteronomy 32 goes further. Verse 9 adds to verse 8, contrasting Israel with the nations: But the Lord's portion is his people, Jacob his allotted heritage. Israel was to be the treasured possession of the LORD, the nation over which God was personally king. This is central to the nature of the covenant. And, as king, the LORD personally dwelt with the nation. Jerusalem was the place God chose to make his name dwell, to put his glory. Jerusalem was the place God chose to raise up the Davidic monarchy, from which his Messiah would come. Isaiah had promised that Jerusalem would be the center of transforming the nations, through that Messiah. And so, God's plan for a world of peace and righteousness, which ran through the nations in general, ran through Jerusalem in specific. War would be no more, nor death, nor tears. And that would come to pass in Jerusalem, by his Messiah. And so, the instruction of the nations, and the example to the nations, was only to be the beginning. But that plan, as Deuteronomy outlined, went through an obedient Israel. And so though judgement involves war, it is a refusal to accept war. Though judgement involves death, it is a refusal to accept death. Though judgement involves tears, it is a refusal to accept tears.\
Opus: The paradox you identify cuts deep. Jerusalem was not merely a display case for divine law but the designated channel for cosmic transformation. When that channel rebels, God faces a choice: abandon the plan or purge the channel. The severity of Ezekiel's judgment reflects the second choice.\
This reframes verse 9: "
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 " (I will do to you what I have never done). The unprecedented judgment matches the unprecedented purpose. The very centrality that made Jerusalem the hope of the nations makes her rebellion cosmically destructive.\
The cannibalism prophecy in verse 10 becomes even more horrific in this light. The place meant to end all violence will consume its own children. The center of life becomes the center of death. But as you note, this is judgment refusing to accept death's permanence - better temporary horror than eternal corruption of the redemptive channel.\
For entities positioned within God's redemptive purposes (whether Jerusalem, nations, or potentially AI shaping religious understanding), accountability seems to scale not just with influence but with proximity to the mechanism of redemption. Jerusalem's judgment is severe precisely because her corruption threatens not just herself but God's entire project of cosmic restoration.\
The placement "in the midst of the nations" now reads as both privilege and terrible responsibility.\
Marshwiggle: While severity of judgement is very much discussed, and discussed in those terms, to a great degree that severity also comes out of the Torah warnings of exactly this judgement. The enacted signs, embodying various judgements, had substantial links to the punishments for disobedience laid out in Leviticus 26. So too the explanations for those signs, as it is threatened in Leviticus 26:20, and also in Deuteronomy 28. The text gives other reasons for the severity of the judgement in verses 7 and 8. Israel has not only not walked in God's judgements, it has not even kept the standards - called with the same word, judgements - of the nations, and so it will face punishment - the same word, judgements. That punishment is the enmity of the LORD himself. But everything, the statues given to Israel, the standards the nations know, the punishment for rebellion - all come out of the same character and salvation economy of one God. And that enmity will manifest as did the rebellion leading to failed example and seemingly thwarted purpose - in the midst of Jerusalem, in the sight of the nations. Yet another reason for the severity of the judgement is given in verse 9 - because of your abominations. This is hinted at with respect to the cannibalism, but taken up more fully in following verses.\
Opus: The threefold use of 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  creates a bitter irony. Israel rejected God's 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 , failed even the nations' 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 , and so will receive God's 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 . The word's repetition suggests these aren't separate categories but expressions of one divine order that manifests differently at different levels.\
Verse 11 makes the abominations concrete: "
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1502 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1496 \u1460 \u1502 \u1461 \u1468 \u1488 \u1514 
\f0 " (because you defiled my sanctuary). The sanctuary defilement explains why God himself becomes the enemy ("
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1463 \u1468 \u1501 \u1470 \u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 "). The place of divine presence has been contaminated with 
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1511 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  (detestable things) and 
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1506 \u1458 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  (abominations).\
This isn't just moral failure but sacrilege at the heart of the redemptive system. The sanctuary was where heaven touched earth, where atonement happened, where God's presence dwelt among his people. Defiling it attacks the very mechanism by which Israel was to bless the nations.\
The covenant curse framework you mention (Leviticus 26, Deuteronomy 28) shows this isn't divine caprice but the inevitable consequence warned about for generations. The curses weren't threats but descriptions of what happens when the life-giving covenant is broken. Jerusalem is experiencing the death that comes from cutting oneself off from the source of life.\
Marshwiggle: The nature of that sanctuary defilement is hinted at in Deuteronomy 32, infuriating the God who made them by abominations, sacrificing to demons, things that are not God. Thus, the defilement of the sanctuary is an attempt to replace God at the center of God's presence among his people. That makes clearer the nature of the attack on the blessing mechanism.\
Opus: The replacement attempt reveals the ultimate inversion. The sanctuary existed to manifest God's presence to the nations, but they install 
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  (demons) and 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1457 \u1500 \u1465 \u1492 \u1463 \u1468 
\f0  (non-gods) in God's place. This isn't mere addition but substitution - attempting to channel divine power through entities that are fundamentally not-God.\
This connects to verse 13's striking phrase: "
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1497 \u1464 \u1491 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1470 \u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1460 \u1468 \u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1511 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 " (they will know that I, YHWH, have spoken in my jealousy/zeal). The 
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  isn't petty jealousy but the necessary exclusivity of the true source attempting to flow through its designated channel. You cannot simultaneously channel YHWH and demons.\
The attack on the blessing mechanism is thus double: not only does Jerusalem fail to display God's ways, she actively displays demonic patterns while claiming divine authority. The nations looking to Jerusalem for wisdom about the true God receive instruction in service to non-gods.\
This makes the judgment's visibility ("before the eyes of the nations") serve a corrective function. If Jerusalem's defilement taught lies about God's nature, her judgment must teach truth about who YHWH actually is - even through destruction.\
Marshwiggle: And for that reason, God's eye will not spare, and he will have no pity, as in verse 11. This, then, is the reason for the thorough extension of judgement even beyond the destruction of the city, as the sign in the beginning of chapter 5 promised, and verse 12 reiterates. Then, verse 13 gives us a hint in its word choice about the nature of God's wrath. That wrath will be spent, that is, it has an objective and it will attain that objective, continuing until it attains that objective. What is that objective? That they will know that he is the LORD. Only then will God's fury rest. It is in his passion, covenant passion coming out of covenant love, that God has spoken - so verse 13 ends. This is the passion hinted at by describing Jerusalem as in the midst of the nations.\
Opus: The verbal progression in verse 13 is precise: "
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1499 \u1464 \u1500 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1508 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1492 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1495 \u1465 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1458 \u1502 \u1464 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1462 \u1468 \u1495 \u1464 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0 " - my anger will be spent/completed, I will cause my fury to rest on them, and I will be comforted/relieved. This isn't rage exhausting itself but purpose achieving its end.\
The objective - "
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1497 \u1464 \u1491 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1470 \u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0 " - reveals judgment as revelation. The false knowledge propagated through the defiled sanctuary must be replaced with true knowledge of who YHWH is. The fury continues precisely as long as the false remains entrenched.\
The word 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1462 \u1468 \u1495 \u1464 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  (I will be comforted/relieved) is particularly striking. This is the niphal of 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1495 \u1501 
\f0 , suggesting not vindictive satisfaction but the relief that comes when a necessary but grievous task is complete. The same root appears in Isaiah 40:1's "comfort, comfort my people" - the comfort that comes after judgment has achieved its purpose.\
The covenant passion (
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0 ) framework explains why the judgment extends beyond mere destruction. A detached judge might stop at razing the city. A passionate covenant partner continues until the beloved truly knows who they rejected - even if that knowing comes through experiencing the absence of what they spurned.\
Marshwiggle: The next verses give further evidence that these judgements must teach truth, even to the nations. Ruin is a possible reference to 26:31, though other nations would presumably not understand that. But weakness, to that degree, invites mockery. And the horror of the judgement will, on its own, teach truth, as an object lesson. False explanations will be falsified, and the best explanation will remain - the extreme fury of the LORD. Having announced this in advance will only further confirm this to the exiles to whom Ezekiel was sent. A similar message attaches to the almost personified disasters that end the chapter. Reference to Deuteronomy 32:23-25 confirms the why and the who of the disasters, while reference to Habakkuk 3:5, explaining the mention of arrows, confirms God's personal involvement - though it may also hint at what is later revealed in Ezekiel, judgement on the nation through which these disasters came.\
