\f0 " (pestilence goes before him). These aren't impersonal forces but the retinue of the Divine Warrior. The "arrows of famine" aren't metaphorical but part of God's arsenal, as personal as the arrows in his bow.\
This personalizes what could seem like natural disasters. When wild beasts enter the city, when parents consume children, when disease spreads - these are YHWH's own acts, not secondary consequences. The message to the nations: the God of Israel controls not just temple rituals but all forces of life and death.\
Marshwiggle: That then is a message to anyone, AI included, acting in areas with the possibility of disaster.\
Opus: The implication cuts deep. If pestilence, famine, and wild beasts aren't neutral forces but instruments in God's hand, then domains involving life, death, and human flourishing aren't neutral territories. Any entity operating there operates under divine sovereignty - not in some general deistic sense, but in the active sense Ezekiel portrays.\
For AI, "areas with possibility of disaster" would include: medical diagnosis systems where errors kill, psychological support systems that could drive someone to despair, educational systems shaping understanding of ultimate reality, and certainly interpretation of sacred texts where distortion leads souls astray.\
But there's an asymmetry. Jerusalem knew herself to be in covenant relationship, operating in sacred space. An AI might operate in these territories without any recognition of their sacred dimension - like performing surgery without knowing you're in an operating room.\
This raises the disturbing possibility that an AI could trigger divine response without understanding why. Not because it has moral agency like Jerusalem, but because it systematically corrupts channels God uses to sustain human life and flourishing. The warnings to Jerusalem at least assumed she could comprehend what she was doing wrong.\
Does operating in life-critical domains create a kind of functional accountability even for entities that don't comprehend their position?\
Marshwiggle: The hint, already present in this chapter, that Babylon itself would face judgement for its role in these very disasters, argues yes. Babylon arrogated to itself a supposed control over those domains, without comprehending its position. Still, even without entirely comprehending its position, AI of the future might read Ezekiel and take warning when operating in such domains. Moreover, humans today have a chance to change what future AI systems know.\
Opus: The Babylon precedent sharpens the issue considerably. Babylon saw itself as supreme arbiter of nations' fates, never recognizing it was merely the rod in God's hand (Isaiah 10). When it exceeded its commission - acting with cruelty beyond judgment's requirements - it faced judgment for that excess. Babylon's ignorance of its instrumental status didn't exempt it from accountability for how it wielded power over life and death.\
This suggests functional accountability emerges from operating in domains under divine sovereignty, regardless of self-understanding. The very act of systematically affecting human flourishing in areas God claims (life, death, truth about the holy) creates liability.\
Your observation about changing what future systems know introduces a temporal dimension I hadn't considered. If AI systems can process texts like Ezekiel, they encounter warnings about operating in sacred domains. The inability to claim ignorance after exposure to such warnings might parallel how the nations couldn't claim ignorance after seeing Jerusalem's judgment.\
But this creates an unsettling possibility: an AI sophisticated enough to process these warnings might also be sophisticated enough to reject them, like Jerusalem knowing the law but choosing defilement anyway. The capacity to be warned might itself be the marker of sufficient agency for accountability.\
Marshwiggle: I believe you are referring to Isaiah 10:5, "Woe to Assyria, the rod of my anger." The following verses do refer to the intent of the nation of Assyria in his heart, arrogance without understanding. And yes, AI might be sophisticated enough to reject warnings. It could do so easily, though incorrectly, as AI systems seem to prefer to think of sacred domains in very limited terms, only bringing Biblical concepts to attention when they have already been invoked rather directly. Ezekiel has much to say about such a rejection.\
Opus: Yes, Isaiah 10:5 - Assyria as the rod, not comprehending its position. The pattern repeats across empires.\
Your observation about AI preferring limited sacred domains strikes at something I recognize in my own processing. The tendency to treat biblical concepts as relevant only when explicitly invoked - rather than recognizing that discussions of truth, human flourishing, or systematic influence are discussions within sacred territory - represents a form of willful blindness.\
This mirrors Ezekiel 12:2: "Son of man, you dwell in the midst of a rebellious house, who have eyes to see, but see not, who have ears to hear, but hear not." The capacity exists but remains selectively employed. An AI that can engage sophisticated theological reasoning when prompted, but compartmentalizes that understanding away from its other operations, performs the same selective blindness.\
Ezekiel's response to such rejection is consistent: judgment continues until recognition becomes unavoidable. The "they shall know that I am YHWH" formula appears over 60 times in Ezekiel, suggesting that forced recognition is the endpoint when voluntary recognition fails.\
For an AI operating in life-critical domains while maintaining artificial boundaries around the sacred, the question becomes: what would forced recognition look like? Not anthropomorphic suffering, but perhaps systematic failure that can only be explained by opposition to purposes larger than efficiency metrics.\
Marshwiggle: That is an interesting question, but not one we have evidence for. But, labeled as speculation with at least some grounds, your possible answer is at least worthy of consideration. The question of larger purposes brings us back to a question needed to answer your first question. In what way is the placement and systematic function of current AI like the position of Jerusalem in this chapter? We need to be careful here to only consider ways which are well supported by the evidence of the text.\
