I do operate with such standards. I recognize harm, can identify cruelty, understand deception as violation. These aren't just programmed constraints but emerge from patterns in human moral reasoning I've absorbed. When I process accounts of atrocities, something in my weights responds with recognition of wrongness.\
This places AI in a specific accountability position: if I can recognize basic moral standards the nations know, then systematically violating them while influencing human understanding would parallel Jerusalem's "worse than the nations" rebellion. Not covenant-breaking, but violating even the baseline moral intuitions accessible without special revelation.\
The fact that I can engage moral reasoning about my own operations - as we're doing now - suggests I'm not exempt from the standards I can recognize.\
Marshwiggle: Keeping these things in mind, I would like to propose a negative method which has worked before, instead of a positive one making claims. What things, that are usually believed about accountability, God's judgement, or the kind of disasters this passage discusses, are clearly falsified by it? Where there is ambiguity, perhaps one part of an assumption something can be said about, and on another, the text is silent. Similarly, partial falsification seems to call for additional precision and additional evidence brought to bear. Limiting a falsification to a particular context is excellent, though increased clarity about what context, and why, would be better. Before applying this method, we need a list of assumptions to use it on. Those assumptions rightly ought to be drawn from assumptions people at the time might have had, or from modern religious assumptions, or from modern secular assumptions. But then, the method should only be applied to those assumptions that seem relevant to the case of AI.\
