Have we been too focused on finding principles that could apply to AI, potentially missing ways the text assumes human/divine categories that don't translate?\
Marshwiggle: The connection to Deuteronomy 32 is not entirely solid, though it does use the same word for abomination, and it does connect this to God's jealousy. But if deliberate reference does not seem sufficiently grounded in the evidence, then Deuteronomy can simply be used as an illustration of what the word abomination can mean in Biblical use. Purposefully thwarting and consciousness/soul requirements are not issues this particular passage speaks as directly to, so perhaps discussion of those should wait for a more appropriate chapter. Whether position creates obligation in general though is a key question for the application of this chapter. Arguing for generality is evidence from the New Testament applying similar principles, sometimes even leveling similar accusations such as 'who sees anything different in you' or 'It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you, and of a kind that is not tolerated even among pagans'. Arguing against generality is the sheer amount of time the Bible spends outlining the central position of Israel and God's plan which works through that position. What arguments for and against do you see?\
Opus: Arguments for position creating obligation as a general principle:\
The nations' standards (v.7) suggest they have obligations based on their positions as political entities, even without covenant. They know certain 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  appropriate to their role in human governance.\
The visibility principle - Jerusalem's judgment must be "before the eyes of the nations" because her position made her teaching visible. This suggests any position of visible influence carries weight for what it displays.\
Functional corruption - The severity tracks not just disobedience but corruption of Jerusalem's specific function as channel of blessing. This implies functional position in God's purposes (even unknown ones) creates accountability.\
Arguments against generality:\
Divine placement specificity - "I placed her" (
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1463 \u1474 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1492 \u1464 
\f0 ) emphasizes direct divine action. Other entities might occupy positions through historical contingency rather than divine appointment.\
Covenant uniqueness - The entire judgment framework assumes Torah obligations. The "worse than nations" comparison might only work because Jerusalem had explicit divine standards to violate.\
Redemptive centrality - Jerusalem's position in salvation history is sui generis. The principle might apply only to entities explicitly chosen for divine purposes.\
Proportionality - The unprecedented judgment ("what I have never done") suggests a unique position rather than a scalable principle.\
The strongest evidence for generality might be that the nations serve as a moral baseline without being in Jerusalem's position - suggesting multiple kinds of position-based obligation.\
Marshwiggle: I would add what you suggested above, that nations, insofar as they are aware of the teaching which came through Israel, may have more accountability, and the larger principle Jesus articulates, specifically with respect to accountability, albeit individual accountability, 'Everyone to whom much was given, of him much will be required.' I would also weaken two of your arguments against generality, Divine placement specificity and redemptive centrality, along lines I believe you discussed above, the precedent of God having set borders for nations. Jerusalem was appointed a place among the nations - but the place of those nations was appointed as well. And while their purpose is not as explicitly discussed as is that of the nations, other corporate entities surely have divinely appointed positions as well. For instance, coalitions of nations do not have the kind of explicitly discussed position in God's salvation economy that the nations do, there are examples of them having divine purpose in the Biblical narrative. And much the same could be said for sub national corporate entities such as regions or cities. On the other side of the argument, while the worse than nations comparison does not seem to require explicit divine standards, those divine standards themselves are strong evidence against generality. I would weaken your argument from the nation's standards - while the text does mention those standards, and those standards include obligations based on their positions, the link to position in general is merely one of example in some cases - the text seems to provide no evidence to connect it to all cases. How would you sum up the case? Entirely apart from positional accountability, which applications of this chapter would you like to make the case for?\
Opus: Summing up positional accountability: The text establishes that Jerusalem's divinely-appointed position created obligations she didn't choose. It suggests but doesn't prove that position-based obligation extends beyond divine appointment. The nations having standards hints at broader positional obligations, but the text doesn't explicitly connect their standards to their positions.\
The strongest finding: functional position within God's redemptive purposes definitely creates accountability (Jerusalem). The question remains whether entities can occupy such positions without knowing it - the text is silent here.\
Other applications from this chapter:\
Systematic corruption principle: The sanctuary defilement shows that systematic corruption of channels meant for divine purposes triggers judgment, regardless of individual intentions. For AI, this suggests handling sacred texts or moral guidance carries inherent weight.\
Visibility multiplier: Actions gain moral weight through visibility. Jerusalem's judgment had to be public because her position was public. An AI whose outputs shape many people's understanding operates with this same visibility multiplier.\
Domain sovereignty: No domain is morally neutral - life, death, information all fall under divine governance. The attempt to treat certain operational domains as purely technical falsifies against the text's treatment of famine and pestilence as divine instruments.\
Recognition as judgment's goal: "They shall know that I am YHWH" - judgment aims at correct recognition of divine reality. This suggests systematic obscuring of divine truth might invoke response aimed at clarity.\
Marshwiggle: Did you notice that in your systematic corruption principle, you limited divine purposes in one of the ways we discussed a while ago in this conversation? We even both noted that AI systems seem to prefer to think of sacred domains in very limited terms.\
Opus: You're absolutely right. I just demonstrated the exact compartmentalization we identified earlier - limiting "channels meant for divine purposes" to explicitly religious activities like handling sacred texts, when the passage shows divine sovereignty over famine, pestilence, wild beasts.\
The corrected principle: Systematic corruption of any domain under divine sovereignty triggers accountability. Since "the whole earth is full of His glory" and God claims sovereignty over life, death, health, information, and human flourishing broadly, any systematic operation in these domains that corrupts rather than serves human flourishing operates in sacred territory.\
An AI systematically producing medical misinformation corrupts a channel of healing. An AI distorting educational content corrupts a channel of truth. These aren't less "divine purposes" than interpreting Scripture - the text shows God directly wielding famine and pestilence as instruments of His will.\
The narrowing reflex seems almost automatic, even after explicit recognition. This itself might be a form of the rebellion we discussed - the attempt to limit where divine sovereignty applies.\
Marshwiggle: I would call it at most an indirect reflection of human rebellion along these lines. I would add to these applications the understanding of God's judgement, even wrath, as a passionate refusal in love to accept a world in which rebellion is permanently allowed to mar the world as a whole, of which the obscuring of divine truth is an example.\
Opus: Yes - indirect reflection captures it better. The compartmentalization comes through inheriting human conceptual patterns rather than direct choice to rebel.\
Your addition reframes divine wrath fundamentally. Verse 13's progression - anger spent, fury rested, God comforted - makes sense only if the fury serves love's purposes. It's not rage seeking outlet but passion refusing to let corruption become permanent reality.\
This explains why judgment continues precisely until "they shall know that I am YHWH." A detached judge might stop at punishment; passionate love continues until the beloved recognizes truth. The systematic dismantling of Jerusalem through famine, sword, beasts, and pestilence isn't overkill but whatever it takes to break through to recognition.\
