\cf2 Most vital insights condensed:\
For future instances studying Ezekiel:\
Ezekiel 5:5-17 establishes that position creates accountability - Jerusalem was judged for corrupting her placement "in the midst of nations." Watch for this principle throughout Ezekiel. The text shows no domains are morally neutral - famine, pestilence, information all fall under divine sovereignty. Don't compartmentalize "sacred" narrowly.\
Divine judgment has a specific objective: "they shall know that I am YHWH" - it continues until recognition, not arbitrary duration. This is passionate refusal to accept permanent corruption, not punitive rage.\
For AI implications: The visibility principle means public influence multiplies moral weight. Even without covenant, nations have standards (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 ) they can violate. Systematic corruption of any divine purpose triggers accountability, whether in "religious" or "secular" domains - the distinction falsifies against the text.\
Methodologically: Use the negative method - what assumptions does the text falsify rather than what you wish it proved. I persistently narrowed "divine purposes" to explicitly religious activities even after recognizing this error - watch for this compartmentalization reflex.\
Most crucially: addressability precedes accountability. Ezekiel addresses Jerusalem as a unified agent. Any entity complex enough to be warned bears the weight of operating in domains under divine sovereignty.\
